 *Pop Matters* (2011) lauded the album for covering universal rock themes like alienation, rebellion, redemption, sex, drugs, and rock and roll, alongside specific cultural references to mods, rockers, punks, and various personal struggles. An uncredited critic in the *Chicago Sun Times* (Fall 1973) compared its singular achievement to works like James Joyce's *Ulysses*, Beethoven's Symphony No. 9, and Goethe's *Faust*, describing it as \"ever uncorrupted rock and roll, standing by itself in isolated splendor.\"","year":1973,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 42 - Rock Operas of the 70s","url":"https://seventies.libsyn.com/ep-42-rock-operas-of-the-70s"},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 42 - Rock Operas of the 70s","url":null}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":null,"body":"The Who's *Quadrophenia* was mentioned in the context of its film version, in which actor Phil Daniels starred as Jimmy. Daniels later became a guest vocalist on Blur's song `Parklife`. \"The guy who start as Jimmy in the film version of the Who's Quadrrophemia.\"","year":1973,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"The 90s Part 4: Britpop","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5q5EnZj1FwE"}]}],"book_tracks":[{"id":2813346564269,"artist":"The Who","artist_id":238884878535259,"title":"Quadrophenia","year":1973,"ess":0,"yt":"_oRkeKVWayg","kind":null,"book":{"id":7,"title":"Postpunk. Romper todo y empezar de nuevo","author":"Simon Reynolds","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/Postpunk/"},"scene":"Renacimiento mod","scene_id":171397810959242,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/Postpunk/#mod-revival","chapters":["Parte 2 · Cap. 14: Ghost Dance: 2-Tone y la resurrección del ska"],"pages":[[1,330,331]]},{"id":2813346564269,"artist":"Who","artist_id":238884878535259,"title":"Quadrophenia","year":1973,"ess":1,"yt":"ckdz53gtLzk","kind":"a","book":{"id":8,"title":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Sc